  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging will remain the dominant weather feature through \nthe middle of the week, supporting another stretch of hot and mainly \ndry weather across South Texas. High temperatures will trend upward \neach day, reaching the middle and upper 90s across the Coastal Bend \nand Coastal Plains, while much of the Brush Country and Rio Grande \nPlains climbs into the triple digits. Afternoon boundary layer \nmixing should mix out some of the higher dewpoints, keeping peak \nheat index values generally between 100 and 110 degrees F. Although \nheat advisory criteria are generally not expected to be met, a \nmoderate HeatRisk will continue for much of the area with localized \nareas of major HeatRisk during the hottest part of the afternoon. \n\nForecast attention shifts to Tropical Depression Two over the \nnortheastern Gulf during the latter half of the week. The latest NHC \nforecast remains largely unchanged, with the system expected to \nmover generally west-northwest across the northern Gulf while \nforecast confidence in its eventual track and intensity remains lower \nthan average. Even though much of the available guidance keeps the \nmore significant impacts east of the Coastal Bend, uncertainty \nremains high enough to maintain low-end rain chances across the \nregion late this week. This accounts for the possibility that outer \nrain bands or deeper tropical moisture could spread farther west \nthan currently indicated. \n\nRegardless of the eventual track, increasing easterly swell is \nexpected to reach the Texas coast late this week. This will likely \nresult in rougher surf, an increased risk of rip currents, and \nincreased risk for coastal flooding. While the probability of \ntropical-storm-force winds along the Middle Texas Coast remains low, \nresidents and mariners should continue to monitor future forecasts \nas the system evolves.",
